  // Initialize grid with starting pattern
  grid[4][5] = 1;
  grid[5][5] = 1;
  grid[6][5] = 1;
}

void loop() {
  int newGrid[rows][cols];
  for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
    for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
      int neighbors = countNeighbors(row, col); // Count live neighbors
      if (grid[row][col] == 1) {
        if (neighbors < 2 || neighbors > 3) {
          newGrid[row][col] = 0; // Die due to under/overpopulation
        } else {
          newGrid[row][col] = 1; // Stay alive
        }
      } else {
        if (neighbors == 3) {
          newGrid[row][col] = 1; // Become alive due to reproduction
        } else {
          newGrid[row][col] = 0; // Stay dead
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Copy newGrid to grid
  for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
    for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
      grid[row][col] = newGrid[row][col];
    }
  }
  delay(1000); // Wait for 1 second before next iteration
}

int countNeighbors(int row, int col) {
  int count = 0;
  for (int i = -1; i <= 1; i++) {
    for (int j = -1; j <= 1; j++) {
      if (i == 0 && j == 0) {
        continue; // Skip the cell itself
      }
      int newRow = row + i;
      int newCol = col + j;
      if (newRow >= 0 && newRow < rows && newCol >= 0 && newCol < cols) {
        count += grid[newRow][newCol];
      }
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```
This Arduino sketch implements Conway's Game of Life on a fixed-size grid (10x10) with a predefined starting pattern. The setup() function initializes the grid with 0s and the loop() function iterates through the Game of Life algorithm. The countNeighbors() function counts the number of live neighbors for a given cell in the grid. The sketch uses a delay of 1 second between each iteration. You can adapt the grid size and starting pattern as needed.

