Here is a simple Python algorithm that performs a DFS on a 2D grid:

```python
def is_connected(grid):
    def dfs(grid, i, j, visited):
        if i < 0 or i >= len(grid) or j < 0 or j >= len(grid[0]) or visited[i][j] or not grid[i][j]:
            return False

        visited[i][j] = True
        up = dfs(grid, i-1, j, visited)
        down = dfs(grid, i+1, j, visited)
        left = dfs(grid, i, j-1, visited)
        right = dfs(grid, i, j+1, visited)

        return up and down and left and right

    visited = [[False]*len(grid[0]) for _ in range(len(grid))]
    for i in range(len(grid)):
        for j in range(len(grid[0])):
            if grid[i][j] and not visited[i][j]:
                if dfs(grid, i, j, visited):
                    return True
                else:
                    return False

    return True
```

This algorithm starts by checking the element at the top left corner of the grid. If it is True, it recursively checks the neighbouring elements. If any of the unvisited neighbours are True, they are marked as visited and checked for connectivity accordingly. The algorithm will continue this process until all elements have been visited. If any unvisited elements remain, it means that there is at least one element that is True but not connected to another True element, and the function will return False. If all elements have been visited by the end, the function will return True, indicating that all True elements are connected.

