To animate an element when it comes into view in CSS, you can use the `:entering` or `:appearing` pseudo-class. This pseudo-class is triggered when an element becomes visible in the viewport due to scrolling.

Here's an example of how to use the `:entering` pseudo-class to animate an element when it comes into view:
```css
html, body {
  height: 200%; /* create a long page to demonstrate scrolling */
}

.element {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; /* position the element in the middle of the page */
  width: 50px;
  height: 50px;
  background-color: blue;
  animation: myAnimation 2s linear;
  animation-fill-mode: forwards;
}

@keyframes myAnimation {
  0% {
    transform: translateY(100%);
  }
  100% {
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}

.element:entering {
  animation-name: myAnimation;
}
```
In this example, the `.element` class defines a div element with a blue background that is positioned in the middle of the page. The `animation` property specifies a named animation called `myAnimation`.

The `@keyframes` rule defines the `myAnimation` animation, which uses the `transform` property to move the element from the top of the page to the bottom over a period of 2 seconds.

The `:entering` pseudo-class is used to trigger the `myAnimation` animation when the element comes into view in the viewport. This is achieved by setting the `animation-name` property to the name of the animation.

Note that the `animation-fill-mode: forwards;` property is used to keep the animation on the :entering state after it finishes.

This will cause the animation to play when the element comes into view in the viewport for the first time.

