When using Google as an Identity Provider (IdP) for a Single Sign-On (SSO) service, such as through the OpenID Connect protocol, you might need to force re-authentication for the user. This is especially useful in scenarios where a user's session has expired or when you want to ensure the user is authenticated with the most recent credentials.

To force re-authentication with Google, you can invoke the `client.getUser()` method within your JavaScript code, which will trigger the authentication flow if the user isn't already authenticated or if their session has expired. You can then handle the returned user profile data and display any relevant information or options.

Here's a general example of how you can force re-authentication using Google as an IdP:

1. Include the necessary libraries in your HTML file:
```html
<script src="https://apis.google.com/js/client.js"></script>
```
1. Initialize the Google client and attach the event listeners:
```javascript
var GoogleClient = chrome.identity.launchWebAuthFlow({
  'url': 'https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v1/userinfo',
  'client_id': '<YOUR_CLIENT_ID>',
  'scopes': ['email']
}, function(response) {
  if (response && response.user) {
    // Handle the user's profile data, including any actions to be taken on refresh
    // ...
  }
});

GoogleClient.addEventListener('token.fetch.success', function(event) {
  if (event.data.access_token) {
    // Handle the received access token, perhaps by making API calls
  }
});

GoogleClient.addEventListener('token.fetch.error', function(event) {
  // Handle errors in fetching tokens
});

GoogleClient.addEventListener('token.refresh.success', function(event) {
  // Handle a successfully refreshed token
});

GoogleClient.addEventListener('token.refresh.error', function(event) {
  // Handle errors in refreshing tokens
});
```
Replace `<YOUR_CLIENT_ID>` with your actual client ID provided by Google during the app registration process.

Please note that this example assumes you are using the Chrome extension-based approach to Google Sign-In. If you are using a different implementation, such as the JavaScript-based `gapi.auth.signIn()` method, the code structure might be slightly different. Make sure to follow the official Google documentation for the specific implementation you're using: [JavaScript implementation](https://developers.google.com/identity/sign-in/web/reference), [Chrome extension implementation](https://developers.google.com/identity/sign-in/chrome-app).

